---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { itemList } from '../../lib/schema';
import { formatDate } from '../../lib/dates';

const PATH = '/guides/';
const guides = (await getCollection('guides')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Guides to Reading a Personality Result"
  description="Plain guides to personality testing: what the research supports, what the percentages mean, and how to get a real result without handing over your email address."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Guides', path: PATH }]}
  schema={[itemList('Upfront guides', guides.map((g) => ({ name: g.data.title, path: `/guides/${g.id}/` })))]}
>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">{guides.length} guides</p>
    <h1>Guides to personality testing</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Written to be useful rather than promotional, including the parts where the research
      does not support what this industry usually claims.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="grid grid-2">
      {guides.map((guide) => (
        <article class="card">
          <h2 style="font-size:1.2rem;margin-top:0">
            <a href={`/guides/${guide.id}/`}>{guide.data.title}</a>
          </h2>
          <p>{guide.data.summary}</p>
          <p class="meta">
            <time datetime={guide.data.updated}>Updated {formatDate(guide.data.updated)}</time>
          </p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tint">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What these guides are for</h2>
      <p>
        Most writing about personality testing falls into one of two camps. The first treats
        every result as revealed truth and builds career advice on top of it. The second
        dismisses the whole field as astrology with a business model. Both are wrong, and the
        second is wrong in a way that costs people something genuinely useful.
      </p>
      <p>
        These guides aim for the defensible middle: what the evidence supports, where it runs
        out, and how to extract value from a result without overreading it.
      </p>

      <h2>Three things worth knowing before you read a result</h2>
      <h3>The percentage matters more than the letter</h3>
      <p>
        A four-letter type where one dimension scored 3% is a completely different result from
        one where every dimension scored 80%, and reporting only the letters hides that. This is
        the most common source of "the test got me wrong" — if you sit near the midpoint on a
        dimension, your letter is close to a coin toss and will legitimately differ between
        tests. <a href="/guides/what-do-personality-test-percentages-mean/">How to read the numbers →</a>
      </p>

      <h3>Type predicts satisfaction better than performance</h3>
      <p>
        These instruments are poor at predicting how well someone will do a job, and noticeably
        better at describing which working conditions would wear them down. That distinction
        matters because most people who leave a role leave over conditions — interruption,
        isolation, pace, politics — rather than because they could not do the work.
        <a href="/guides/are-personality-tests-accurate/">What the research actually supports →</a>
      </p>

      <h3>The gate is a business decision, not a technical one</h3>
      <p>
        Nothing about scoring a personality test requires an email address. When a test asks for
        one after the questions rather than before, that placement is deliberate: it is the
        highest-conversion moment the site will ever have with you.
        <a href="/guides/personality-test-without-email/">Why the email gate exists →</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Start with a result</h2>
      <p>
        These guides make considerably more sense alongside your own numbers. The
        <a href="/tests/personality-type/">personality type test</a> takes about nine minutes and
        reports preference strength on each dimension rather than just handing you four letters.
        If your real question is what work to do, the <a href="/tests/career-fit/">career fit test</a>
        measures vocational interest, which predicts job satisfaction considerably better than
        type does.
      </p>
      <p>
        How the tests themselves are constructed — item wording, keying balance, and the scoring
        maths — is set out on the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
